              {
                "id": "R4-unlink-partial-listing",
                "lens": "resilience",
                "location": "engine/runtime/pi_agents.go:198-218",
                "severity": "WARNING",
                "claim": "unlinkPackageAgents now only errors when both the package listing and the home links listing fail. If the package agents directory is unreadable for a reason other than absence, and the home listing succeeds, the package error is silently dropped and uninstall proceeds with only the owned links found in the home. That is a graceful degradation, and the owned links are still removed, so the harm is limited to a lost diagnostic. Conversely, if the home directory listing fails but the package listing succeeds, linksErr is also dropped and links of agents no longer in the package could be missed without any report.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/runtime/pi_agents.go:198-218"
                ],
                "evidence_class": "inferential",
                "causal_disposition": "introduced"
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
              "Inspected all 10 manifest paths in the candidate patch.",
              "The change in engine/runtime/pi_agents.go fixes the earlier review's finding. Uninstall now removes overlay-owned links even when the package agents directory is gone, via ownedAgentLinks, which checks Readlink equality through agentLinkState, so foreign links and regular files are left untouched.",
              "The new test in engine/runtime/pi_agents_test.go covers the missing-package case and asserts that an unrelated file in the home agents directory is unchanged.",
              "ownedAgentLinks returns nil, nil when the home agents directory does not exist, so Uninstall on a clean home does not fail.",
              "The Check error header change in engine/agentgen/agentgen.go and the pinned test updates are consistent with each other and change only message text.",
              "The receipt JSON files and the task notes are documentation and record data, with no runtime behavior.",
              "No BLOCKER or CRITICAL resilience defect was found."
            ]
          }
